\section{Part XIV or Part XV}{

The GTDC file is named "Part14" and this table "P15". They are the same
schedule: grants and contributions \strong{paid} during the year. The IRS
renumbered the 990-PF supplementary-information part, and the e-file
extracts follow the current numbering. \code{PF-P15-T01} is the repeating
grant-level table; \code{PF-P15-T00} is the one-row-per-return parent;
\code{PF-P15-T02} is grants \emph{approved for future payment}, which is a different
population and is \strong{not} what this project matches.
}

\section{2025 drops the recipient-status column}{

\code{PF_15_G_PAID_RECIP_STAT} -- the GTDC \code{SIGOCPYRFSTA}, "foundation status of
recipient" -- is present every year 2009-2024 and \strong{absent from 2025}. One
column removed; the other 29 are identical. The IRS revised Form 990-PF and
dropped that box from Part XV line 3, so this is permanent and 2026 onward
will not have it either.

It matters more than a missing field normally would, because
\code{\link[=pf_standardize_status]{pf_standardize_status()}} reads it and it is the only input to the scope
decision. Measured rather than assumed: with the column absent every row
classifies as \code{other_unknown} and \strong{\code{in_scope} stays \code{TRUE}}, because the
recipient-type filter flags individuals and government bodies rather than
dropping them and only a foreign address removes a row. So 2025 grants are
matched normally; what is lost is the ability to \emph{crosstab} them by
recipient type, and any such breakdown must exclude 2025 or report it
separately rather than showing a year that is 100\% \code{other_unknown} and
letting a reader conclude the data is broken.
}

\section{Column naming}{

\code{\link[=pf_efilepf_build]{pf_efilepf_build()}} emits the \strong{GTDC column names}, not the e-file ones.
That is deliberate: \code{\link[=pf_prepare_grants]{pf_prepare_grants()}} reads \code{SIGOCPYRBNBN1},
\code{SIGOCPYRFSTA} and friends by name, as do the recipient-type and scope
rules, so emitting the historical schema means the entire downstream
pipeline, every benchmark and every frozen artifact keeps working untouched.
\code{\link[=pf_efilepf_map]{pf_efilepf_map()}} is the mapping, exported so the translation is auditable
rather than buried.
}

